  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1234 AM CDT Sat Oct 10 2026\n\nVery warm, and even borderline hot, temperatures this weekend \nthrough Monday as a southwest downslope plume overspreads the \nCentral and Southern High Plains. Monday still looks to be the \nwarmest day with widespread highs in the lower 90s. The record high \nfor Dodge City on October 12th (Monday) is 94F, which is around the \n33rd percentile off the 01Z run of the NBM, so the record high is \nwell within the envelope of \"reasonable\" forecast high range (25-\n75th percentile) based on NBM percentile data. Increased challenges \nin the forecast exist after Monday as a fairly shallow cold front \npushes south across Nebraska into Kansas Monday Night into Tuesday. \nThe front position late Tuesday remains highly uncertain as it will \nlikely slow down quite a bit facing a strong southwest flow pattern \naloft. That being said, the air mass behind the front early next \nweek will be quite cool, so some semblance of a density current will \nexist, and the SLP tendencies behind the front will probably be \nfairly high which would keep the front moving, perhaps more so than \ncurrent models show. Thus, this will have an impact on afternoon \nhighs Tuesday as well as placement of greatest severe weather risk.  \n\nRegardless of the severe weather risk Tuesday afternoon/evening, \nrain chances still look pretty good as deep moisture will move up \ninto the Southern High Plains thanks to remnants of Pacific tropical \nsystem become infused in the southwest flow pattern aloft as a polar \ntrough/upper low moves across the Rockies. Models are still all over \nthe place with evolution of the upper level jet/trough/upper low \nmoving across the central CONUS mid next week, so expect further \nadjustments in precipitation chances/timing going into the late \nTuesday through Thursday time frame.",
